WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.406
Picture a small print shop.

2
00:00:04.406 --> 00:00:09.273
Behind the counter sits a binder of procedure sheets, like how to time a job.

3
00:00:09.273 --> 00:00:10.537
A clerk takes a job.

4
00:00:10.537 --> 00:00:16.667
She can pass it to a colleague who knows the sheet, with a slip for the one step only she can do.

5
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:21.976
Or she can photocopy the sheet into the job's own folder, and do every step herself.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.453
The copy is quicker.

7
00:00:23.453 --> 00:00:24.894
It costs paper, though.

8
00:00:24.894 --> 00:00:28.465
And it never hears about corrections to the master sheet.

9
00:00:28.465 --> 00:00:31.848
Keep those three in mind: quicker, paper, corrections.

10
00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:35.588
Now a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:35.588 --> 00:00:39.196
Everybody says: mark small helpers inline, it's faster.

12
00:00:39.196 --> 00:00:40.311
Faster than what?

13
00:00:40.311 --> 00:00:44.313
What does it even cost to pass one tiny lambda to a function?

14
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.442
Let's find out with a helper the shop's app uses everywhere.

15
00:00:49.442 --> 00:00:53.170
It runs one step of a job and prints how long it took.

16
00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.139
Today's step: how much ink does a job need?

17
00:00:56.139 --> 00:01:00.281
The shop's rule of thumb is 3 ml of ink for every 100 pages.

18
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:06.444
The part in braces is a lambda: a small function, written in place and passed as a value.

19
00:01:06.444 --> 00:01:09.784
It uses pages, a variable from outside its braces.

20
00:01:09.784 --> 00:01:14.259
A lambda that carries outside values like that is called a closure.

21
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:19.670
So, besides the multiply and the divide, what extra work does one call do?

22
00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:20.795
Pause and guess.

23
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:25.365
Three things.

24
00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:30.136
One object: the JVM builds a small object that holds a copy of pages.

25
00:01:30.136 --> 00:01:37.879
One call through an interface, a type that only names its methods: the helper calls that object's invoke method.

26
00:01:37.879 --> 00:01:39.054
And one unboxing.

27
00:01:39.054 --> 00:01:48.180
The helper works for any result type, so the answer travels back as a plain Object, and has to be turned back into a primitive long.

28
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.050
In the shop, this is the clerk handing the job to a colleague.

29
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.699
The helper, timedNotInline, is the colleague.

30
00:01:54.699 --> 00:01:58.467
The lambda is the slip, with the clerk's own step written on it.

31
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:02.059
And invoke is the colleague calling her back to do that step.

32
00:02:02.967 --> 00:02:09.999
You could skip all three by hand: paste the helper's body into the caller, with the lambda's body in the middle.

33
00:02:09.999 --> 00:02:10.564
It works.

34
00:02:10.564 --> 00:02:13.013
Now picture doing that in fifty places.

35
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:16.561
Wouldn't it be nice if someone did the pasting for us?

36
00:02:16.561 --> 00:02:18.471
Well, the compiler is happy to.

37
00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.552
It never gets bored, and it never gets paper cuts.

38
00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:25.084
Write inline in front of fun, and keep the same body.

39
00:02:25.084 --> 00:02:33.721
Now, at every call site, each place in the code that calls the function, the compiler copies in the function's body and the lambda's body.

40
00:02:33.721 --> 00:02:40.731
That's the photocopy: the whole procedure lands in the job's own folder, with the clerk's step written in place.

41
00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:44.022
Let's see if that's really true.

42
00:02:44.022 --> 00:02:50.966
javap, a tool in the JDK, prints the bytecode in a class file: the instructions the JVM runs.

43
00:02:50.966 --> 00:02:56.117
The lesson's demo has two tiny functions, inkNotInline and inkInline.

44
00:02:56.333 --> 00:03:02.064
Which one builds an object with invokedynamic, and which one does the multiply itself?

45
00:03:02.064 --> 00:03:03.131
Pause and guess.

46
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.801
inkNotInline is seven instructions.

47
00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:15.018
invokedynamic builds a Function0, Kotlin's interface for a function with no parameters.

48
00:03:15.018 --> 00:03:16.161
That's the slip.

49
00:03:16.161 --> 00:03:18.305
invokestatic calls the helper.

50
00:03:18.305 --> 00:03:19.949
That's handing it over.

51
00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:23.236
Then checkcast and longValue unbox the answer.

52
00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:25.440
inkInline has none of that.

53
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.363
No invokedynamic, no call to the helper.

54
00:03:28.363 --> 00:03:34.939
The multiply, lmul, and the divide, ldiv, sit right inside, between two calls to nanoTime.

55
00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:37.424
Just like our hand-pasted version.

56
00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:43.699
Run them, and they agree: 12,000 pages need 360 ml of ink.

57
00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:46.342
So, should we inline everything?

58
00:03:46.342 --> 00:03:48.158
Look further down inkInline.

59
00:03:48.158 --> 00:03:54.318
The finally block is there twice: once for the normal exit, and once for the exit by exception.

60
00:03:54.318 --> 00:03:57.691
Every caller carries its own copy of the whole body.

61
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.755
The good news: no slip, no call, no box.

62
00:04:01.755 --> 00:04:04.910
The bad news: every folder gets thicker.

63
00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:09.564
A 40-line helper called from 200 places becomes 200 copies.

64
00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:11.805
So here's the rule.

65
00:04:11.805 --> 00:04:15.188
inline is for small functions that take lambdas.

66
00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:23.009
The standard library is full of them: forEach, map, filter, let, apply, use, repeat and require are all inline.

67
00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:25.726
And remember the corrections?

68
00:04:25.726 --> 00:04:30.229
A photocopy never hears about them, and neither does an inlined copy.

69
00:04:30.229 --> 00:04:33.036
A caller gets its copy when it is compiled.

70
00:04:33.036 --> 00:04:39.758
In the lesson's test, an app built against version 1 of a library ran with version 2, and printed this.

71
00:04:39.758 --> 00:04:42.108
The plain function ran the new code.

72
00:04:42.108 --> 00:04:44.588
The inline one still ran its old copy.

73
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.044
And here's where the picture breaks.

74
00:04:48.044 --> 00:04:52.990
Java can't photocopy anything, because javac can't inline Kotlin code.

75
00:04:52.990 --> 00:05:01.399
To Java, timed is an ordinary static method that takes a Function0, and a Java lambda becomes an object like any other.

76
00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:03.660
Java always hands over the slip.

77
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:08.920
One more thing changes once the step sits in the clerk's own folder.

78
00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:13.082
If it says stop here, she stops the whole job, not just her step.

79
00:05:13.082 --> 00:05:17.371
So a return inside an inlined lambda leaves more than you'd expect.

80
00:05:17.371 --> 00:05:18.908
That's the next section.

81
00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:21.217
So, faster than what?

82
00:05:21.217 --> 00:05:24.512
Faster than a slip, a call and a box on every use.

83
00:05:24.512 --> 00:05:28.267
inline copies the helper and its lambda into each caller.

84
00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:31.167
Keep it for small helpers that take lambdas.

85
00:05:31.367 --> 00:05:32.326
Quick check.

86
00:05:32.326 --> 00:05:39.199
The shop also has toSheets, which turns a page count into sheets with (pages + 1) / 2.

87
00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:40.718
No lambda in sight.

88
00:05:40.718 --> 00:05:42.316
Should it be inline?

89
00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:45.701
No.

90
00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:49.326
There's no slip to remove, so there's nothing to gain.

91
00:05:49.326 --> 00:05:52.347
The compiler even says so, with this warning.
